--TEST--
`Core\Json::encode` ends a cycle at the first repeat on the path and names the chain that closed it, while a value merely held twice is written twice and depth alone ends nothing — `rule:classes/an-encoder-ends-a-cycle-by-identity`, spec § 6
--FILE--
<?nvs
use Core\Json;

#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Node {
    public function constructor(public string $name, public ?Node $next) {}
}

#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Tree {
    public function constructor(public string $name, public array<Tree> $kids) {}
}

#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Pair {
    public function constructor(public Node $left, public Node $right) {}
}

// The identities carried are the ancestor chain and never everything seen: one
// object two properties both hold is shared rather than cyclic, and a format
// with no way to spell sharing has no answer but to write it out twice.
Node $one = new Node("one", null);
echo Json::encode(new Pair($one, $one)), "\n";

// The first repeat on the current path ends the walk, and what the message
// names is the property chain that closed it rather than a count of levels.
Node $self = new Node("self", null);
$self->next = $self;
try {
    var $writtenSelf = Json::encode($self);
    echo "not refused=", $writtenSelf, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $direct) {
    echo $direct->message, "\n";
}

Node $a = new Node("a", null);
Node $b = new Node("b", null);
$a->next = $b;
$b->next = $a;
try {
    var $writtenMutual = Json::encode($a);
    echo "not refused=", $writtenMutual, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $mutual) {
    echo $mutual->message, "\n";
}

// A cycle closing through an array names the element's position, spelled as a
// decode's own dotted issue path is.
Tree $root = new Tree("root", []);
$root->kids = [$root];
try {
    var $writtenRoot = Json::encode($root);
    echo "not refused=", $writtenRoot, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $viaList) {
    echo $viaList->message, "\n";
}

// The other side: depth alone refuses nothing. A chain far longer than any
// cycle here, holding a distinct object at every level, is written out whole —
// what ends a walk is the repeat and never the length, which is the difference
// the depth counter alone could not tell.
Node $deep = new Node("leaf", null);
int $built = 0;
while ($built < 100) {
    $deep = new Node("n", $deep);
    $built = $built + 1;
}
echo "deep=", $built, " len=", Core\Str::length(Json::encode($deep)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
{"left":{"name":"one","next":null},"right":{"name":"one","next":null}}
Core\Json::encode(): a value that holds itself has no JSON encoding — `next` is a value it is already inside
Core\Json::encode(): a value that holds itself has no JSON encoding — `next.next` is a value it is already inside
Core\Json::encode(): a value that holds itself has no JSON encoding — `kids.0` is a value it is already inside
deep=100 len=2027
